5. SCREENSHOTS (uploaded to the visual-proof release, linked on the PR)
================================================================================

02-before-502.png
  http://127.0.0.1:18099/ -> HTTP 502 in 0.11s
  Caddyfile.owui as it stands on main, upstream unresolvable. A browser
  navigating during a recreate gets an error page.

03-after-recovered.png
  http://127.0.0.1:18098/ -> HTTP 200 in 4.17s
  Same file with the retry, same unresolvable upstream at request time, the
  container appearing mid-hold. The browser gets the page.

The upstream behind 03 is a deliberately labelled throwaway `caddy file-server`
standing in for open-webui, not the Hive product, and the page says so in its
own body. The real chat UI could not be photographed through this harness for a
reason unrelated to the change: Open WebUI redirects to SSO and Supabase
rejects a redirect_uri on a loopback harness origin with HTTP 400, so the SPA
never renders on any origin but the real one. The live-upstream check in
section 4 covers that half at the HTTP level instead.

Both URLs are loopback harness ports with no query string. No credential,
token, invite or callback value appears in either image or in this log.

================================================================================
6. WHAT IS NOT PROVEN HERE
================================================================================

The after-rate on the real box cannot be measured before this merges, because
the Caddyfile only takes effect on the next deploy. The before-rate is section
1 (55 on chat, 3 on console, 24h). The claim this change makes is section 4: a
request arriving in a recreate window is served rather than refused. The rate
should be re-counted with the section 1 commands over a comparable window once
this has been deployed for a day.

This change does not remove the outage. Every deploy that recreates open-webui
still takes chat down for 4 to 40 seconds, and there were more than forty
deploys in the measured day. It makes that window invisible to most requests. A
health-gated cutover is the thing that would remove it, and it is a separate
and much larger change.

